Interface addresses|ip -br address|Shows concise interface states and assigned addresses.|Confirm the source interface and address before tracing a path.|UP is link state; address suffixes show prefix length. An address alone does not prove a usable route.|eth0 UP 192.0.2.20/24|Read-only Linux command; example values are documentation addresses.
Route selection|ip route get 192.0.2.10|Asks the kernel which route it would use for one destination.|Check next hop, output interface and selected source address.|via is next hop, dev is interface and src is chosen local source.|192.0.2.10 via 192.0.2.1 dev eth0 src 192.0.2.20|Read-only; substitute the actual destination and consider policy routing.
Neighbor state|ip neigh show|Displays the kernel neighbor table.|Find failed local next-hop resolution.|REACHABLE is recently confirmed; STALE can be normal; FAILED indicates unsuccessful resolution.|192.0.2.1 dev eth0 lladdr 00:11:22:33:44:55 REACHABLE|Read-only; use ip -6 neigh for IPv6-specific inspection.
DNS answer|dig example.com A|Queries DNS for IPv4 address records.|Inspect response code, returned records and TTLs.|status NXDOMAIN means the name does not exist in that response; ANSWER records include remaining TTL.|example.com. 300 IN A 192.0.2.10|Read-only network query; DNS results are illustrative.
Specific resolver|dig @192.0.2.53 example.com A|Directs a DNS query to a chosen resolver.|Compare two resolvers during inconsistent resolution.|The SERVER field confirms the queried resolver; compare status, answer and TTL, not only the final address.|SERVER: 192.0.2.53#53|Replace the documentation address with an authorized resolver; a timeout does not prove the record is absent.
DNS authority chain|dig +trace example.com|Walks DNS delegation from root toward authoritative servers.|Locate a delegation or authority discrepancy.|Each referral identifies the next authoritative servers. Failure can also reflect blocked outbound DNS from the test host.|NS referrals followed by an authoritative answer|Generates multiple DNS queries; does not test every application's resolver cache.
HTTP response|curl -I --max-time 10 https://example.com|Requests HTTP headers using HEAD with a total time limit.|Inspect status, redirects and cache headers without downloading the body.|The status line identifies response class; Location identifies redirect destination. Some services treat HEAD differently from GET.|HTTP/2 200|Read-only request but still reaches the service; use a known safe endpoint.
HTTP timing|curl -sS -o /dev/null --max-time 10 -w 'dns=%{time_namelookup} connect=%{time_connect} tls=%{time_appconnect} total=%{time_total}\n' https://example.com|Measures cumulative milestones for one HTTP request.|Distinguish name resolution, connection, TLS and total duration.|Times are cumulative seconds from request start; subtract adjacent milestones for stage estimates.|dns=0.010 connect=0.030 tls=0.070 total=0.120|Use an authorized read endpoint; one sample is not a latency distribution.
TLS verification|openssl s_client -connect example.com:443 -servername example.com -verify_return_error -verify_hostname example.com|Connects with TLS, supplies SNI and validates certificate identity.|Inspect chain, negotiated protocol and verification errors.|Check verification result and hostname; a completed encrypted handshake without verification is insufficient.|Verification: OK|Uses the system trust configuration; never interpret disabling verification as a production fix.
TCP socket detail|ss -ti state established|Shows established TCP connections with internal transport information.|Investigate retransmission, congestion and round-trip behavior.|rtt estimates round-trip time; retrans fields and congestion information provide transport clues, not application health.|ESTAB ... rtt:12.1/2.0 ...|Read-only; field availability depends on kernel and iproute2 version.
Bounded packet capture|tcpdump -ni eth0 -c 50 'host 192.0.2.10 and tcp port 443'|Captures up to fifty matching packets on one interface.|Check whether SYNs, replies and resets traverse the observed point.|S is SYN, S. is SYN-ACK, R is reset; compare direction and timing.|IP client.port > server.443: Flags [S]|Requires capture privileges; use a scoped filter and protect captured metadata or payloads.
Path probe|traceroute -n -m 15 example.com|Probes a path with increasing hop limits, bounded to fifteen hops.|Locate routing changes and broad failure boundaries.|Asterisks mean no probe reply, not necessarily forwarded traffic loss. Routers may suppress or deprioritize replies.|1 192.0.2.1 1.2 ms ...|Generates diagnostic traffic; confirm tool availability and authorized target.
Interface counters|ip -s link show dev eth0|Shows link state and interface packet counters.|Check for receive/transmit errors or drops on the affected interface.|Compare RX/TX errors and dropped counters over time; cumulative totals alone do not locate the cause.|RX: bytes packets errors dropped missed mcast|Read-only Linux iproute2 command; replace eth0 with the actual interface.
IPv4 route table|ip -4 route show table main|Lists routes in the main IPv4 routing table.|Inspect next hops and prefixes used for ordinary routing.|default covers unmatched destinations; via is the next hop and dev is the outgoing interface.|default via 192.0.2.1 dev eth0|Read-only; policy routing may select other tables.
Policy routing rules|ip rule show|Lists rules that choose routing tables or actions.|Investigate paths that cannot be explained by the main route table.|Lower numeric priorities are examined first; selectors can depend on source address, marks or other attributes.|32766: from all lookup main|Read-only; correlate selected rules with the relevant packet source and mark.
IPv6 route selection|ip -6 route get 2001:db8::10|Asks the kernel which route it would use for an IPv6 destination.|Check IPv6 source address and next hop separately from IPv4.|via identifies a router and dev the interface; an unreachable error means no usable route was selected.|2001:db8::10 via fe80::1 dev eth0 src 2001:db8::2|Read-only; documentation addresses are placeholders and do not represent reachable test targets.
IPv6 neighbors|ip -6 neigh show|Lists IPv6 neighbor cache entries.|Inspect local-neighbor resolution when IPv6 traffic fails.|REACHABLE and STALE are normal states; INCOMPLETE or FAILED can indicate unsuccessful resolution.|fe80::1 dev eth0 lladdr 02:00:00:00:00:01 router STALE|Read-only; investigate link and ICMPv6 behavior rather than flushing caches reflexively.
Bounded reachability test|ping -c 4 -W 2 192.0.2.10|Sends a limited set of ICMP echo requests.|Measure basic reachability and response timing to an authorized endpoint.|Packet loss and RTT summarize replies to these probes; no reply does not prove TCP service failure.|4 packets transmitted, 4 received, 0% packet loss|Sends network traffic; replace the example address and respect networks that filter ICMP.
Path MTU investigation|tracepath -n 192.0.2.10|Probes a path and reports discovered path-MTU information.|Investigate a tunnel or packet-size-related connectivity issue.|pmtu reports an observed path-MTU estimate; missing hops may reflect filtered probes.|1: 192.0.2.1 pmtu 1500|Sends probes; requires tracepath. Results describe the probed path and may differ from application traffic.
Repeated hop statistics|mtr --report --report-cycles 10 --no-dns example.com|Collects a bounded report of hop responses and timing.|Compare path behavior during an authorized connectivity investigation.|Loss% at an intermediate hop alone can reflect probe deprioritization; examine whether loss persists to the destination.|HOST Loss% Snt Last Avg Best Wrst StDev|Sends repeated probes; use a real authorized target and avoid treating every nonresponsive router as a fault.
TCP port probe|nc -vz -w 3 192.0.2.10 443|Attempts a TCP connection without sending an application payload.|Check whether a specific host and port accepts a connection.|Succeeded confirms transport acceptance only; refused and timed out imply different failure paths.|Connection to 192.0.2.10 443 port [tcp/https] succeeded!|OpenBSD-style netcat syntax; probe only authorized targets. TLS and application health remain untested.
UDP listener view|ss -lunp|Lists listening UDP sockets with numeric endpoints.|Verify a UDP receiver's local binding.|Local Address:Port shows the interface scope; UDP has no TCP-style established handshake.|UNCONN 0 0 0.0.0.0:8125 0.0.0.0:*|Read-only; process details may require elevated visibility.
Socket summary|ss -s|Summarizes socket counts by protocol and state.|Assess connection churn and resource pressure before inspecting individual sockets.|TCP totals distinguish established, closed and timewait counts; counts are not request throughput.|TCP: 120 (estab 40, closed 60, orphaned 0, timewait 60)|Read-only; compare with trends and ephemeral-port capacity.
Connection tracking totals|conntrack -S|Displays connection-tracking statistics from the kernel.|Investigate state-table allocation failures on a Linux firewall or NAT host.|Counters such as insert_failed and drop need time-correlated interpretation; cumulative values may include old incidents.|cpu=0 found=0 invalid=2 insert_failed=0 drop=0|Read-only but usually privileged; requires conntrack-tools and an applicable netfilter setup.
Firewall ruleset review|nft list ruleset|Prints the active nftables ruleset.|Trace filtering and NAT logic before making rule changes.|Tables contain chains and hooks; rule order and verdicts determine processing. Counters appear only when configured.|table inet filter { chain input { ... } }|Read-only and often requires privilege; rules can disclose internal network policy.
Link negotiation|ethtool eth0|Reports supported link modes and negotiated link properties.|Check speed, duplex and carrier when physical-link behavior is suspect.|Speed and Duplex describe negotiation; Link detected indicates carrier, not end-to-end service reachability.|Speed: 1000Mb/s Duplex: Full Link detected: yes|Read-only form; support varies by driver and virtual interface.
Driver counters|ethtool -S eth0|Displays driver-specific network interface statistics.|Find hardware or queue errors not exposed in the generic interface summary.|Counter names and units vary by driver; compare changes during the affected interval.|rx_crc_errors: 0|Read-only; consult the device/driver documentation before interpreting a nonzero counter.
Network namespace inventory|ip netns list|Lists named network namespaces known to iproute2.|Identify separate network contexts for a scoped investigation.|Each line is a namespace name, optionally with an identifier; unnamed container namespaces may not appear.|blue (id: 0)|Read-only; absence here does not mean no network namespaces exist.
Namespace route inspection|ip netns exec NAMESPACE ip route show|Runs a route inspection inside a named network namespace.|Compare the failing workload's route view with the host's.|Output is that namespace's routing table; host routes may differ completely.|default via 10.0.0.1 dev veth0|Usually requires privilege. The selected command is read-only, but netns exec can run arbitrary commands.
IPv6 DNS lookup|dig example.com AAAA|Queries DNS for IPv6 address records.|Separate missing IPv6 records from client routing or listener problems.|ANSWER contains AAAA records and TTLs; status and authority sections explain empty or negative responses.|example.com. 300 IN AAAA 2001:db8::10|Read-only query traffic; returned documentation values are illustrative.
Authoritative DNS servers|dig example.com NS|Queries the name-server records for a domain.|Identify authoritative DNS infrastructure during delegation checks.|NS records name servers, not necessarily their addresses; compare parent delegation and authoritative responses if needed.|example.com. 3600 IN NS ns1.example.com.|Read-only DNS traffic; a recursive answer may be cached.
DNS negative-response context|dig missing.example.com SOA|Queries a name and displays DNS response status and authority data.|Inspect the evidence behind a cached negative answer.|NXDOMAIN means the name does not exist in that answer; an SOA in AUTHORITY contributes to negative caching semantics.|status: NXDOMAIN ... AUTHORITY SECTION ... SOA|Read-only; distinguish NXDOMAIN from NOERROR with an empty answer.
DNS over TCP|dig +tcp example.com A|Sends a DNS lookup using TCP instead of the usual UDP transport.|Compare behavior when DNS responses are truncated or a network permits only one transport.|The answer and status should be interpreted normally; transport success does not prove UDP works.|Query time: 12 msec; SERVER: 192.0.2.53#53|Sends DNS traffic; use the same resolver when comparing transports.
System resolver status|resolvectl status|Displays systemd-resolved global and per-link resolver settings.|Find which DNS servers and routing domains apply to a host.|Current DNS Server and DNS Domains are per-link context; split DNS can send names to different servers.|Link 2 (eth0): DNS Servers: 192.0.2.53|Read-only; applies only where systemd-resolved manages resolution.
Resolver cache statistics|resolvectl statistics|Shows systemd-resolved query and cache statistics.|Check whether local caching may be relevant to repeated lookup behavior.|Cache Hits and Cache Misses are aggregate counts, not proof that one hostname has a particular cached value.|Current Cache Size: 12 Cache Hits: 100 Cache Misses: 20|Read-only; available only with systemd-resolved.
Verbose HTTP exchange|curl -v --max-time 10 https://example.com/health|Performs an HTTPS request with connection and protocol diagnostics.|Locate whether failure occurs at DNS, TCP, TLS or HTTP handling.|Lines beginning with > are sent headers and < received headers; TLS errors can occur before an HTTP status exists.|< HTTP/2 200|Sends a request; verbose output may reveal headers. Do not include production tokens in shared traces.
Test a chosen HTTPS endpoint|curl --resolve example.com:443:192.0.2.10 --max-time 10 https://example.com/health|Overrides name-to-address resolution for one host/port while retaining the URL hostname.|Test a candidate backend before changing DNS.|The connection uses the supplied IP while Host and TLS hostname checks use example.com; status is the backend response.|HTTP response body from the selected endpoint|Sends traffic to the selected address; use authorized real values. Do not disable certificate validation to hide failures.
Inspect certificate metadata|openssl x509 -in server.pem -noout -subject -issuer -dates|Reads identity, issuer and validity dates from a PEM certificate.|Inspect a certificate file before deployment or during expiry diagnosis.|notBefore/notAfter delimit validity; subject and issuer alone do not establish trust or hostname coverage.|notAfter=Nov 23 10:00:00 2026 GMT|Read-only local inspection; use separate verification for chain trust and SAN hostname matching.
Read a packet capture|tcpdump -nn -r capture.pcap -c 20|Decodes a limited number of packets from a saved capture.|Review protocol endpoints and flags without generating new traffic.|-nn prevents name/service translation; timestamps and TCP flags describe observed packets, not necessarily complete conversations.|10:00:00 IP 192.0.2.2.53000 > 192.0.2.10.443: Flags [S]|Read-only file parsing; captures may contain sensitive payloads and should be handled as incident evidence.
Capture TCP resets|tcpdump -ni eth0 -c 20 'tcp[tcpflags] & tcp-rst != 0'|Captures a bounded set of TCP reset packets on an interface.|Identify endpoints actively resetting connections.|Flags [R] or [R.] show resets; source address identifies the observed sender, which may be a middlebox.|192.0.2.10.443 > 192.0.2.2.53000: Flags [R.]|Passive capture often requires privilege. Scope to the incident and protect captured data.
